bookmarking in terms of providing a way for people to sort of save a certain state of your shiny

00:50:37.754 --> 00:50:48.475
application. Because actually adding shiny meta integration into a shiny app does involve kind of

00:50:48.475 --> 00:50:53.754
understanding this sort of custom way of meta programming, where you're supposed to use this

00:50:53.754 --> 00:51:00.715
dot operator, which conceptually, it's quite similar to bang bang from Arlang.

00:51:01.835 --> 00:51:04.555
Okay. But it's different. It's actually

00:51:06.555 --> 00:51:13.435
a little bit more complex in terms of understanding exactly what it's doing. So there's a whole

00:51:14.394 --> 00:51:19.514
article on this, on how to actually get your shiny app to generate code.

00:51:20.394 --> 00:51:26.955
But the main idea is you basically any sort of reactive read, like an input dollar variable,

00:51:27.754 --> 00:51:35.355
or a read of a reactive expression, like this bear, any sort of reactive read like that,

00:51:35.355 --> 00:51:47.435
if you just wrap that read in this dot operator, that is kind of one of the two main pieces of

00:51:47.435 --> 00:51:55.994
adding shiny meta integration to your application. So that, you know, this example here is showing,

00:51:55.994 --> 00:52:01.195
you know, how I can choose different variables to choose a summary, which is shown in this

00:52:01.195 --> 00:52:11.355
render print output here. But then if you read an output, like this output dollar summary

00:52:11.355 --> 00:52:18.394
inside of an expand chain context that has been marked appropriately with these dot operators,

00:52:20.235 --> 00:52:29.195
then the shiny app will, like the result of this expand chain call will be the R code that shiny

00:52:29.195 --> 00:52:38.874
is essentially used to produce the output in the application. So that's a real minimal example of

00:52:38.874 --> 00:52:46.715
how, you know, you can go from targeting a specific output where you want to provide reproducible code

00:52:47.355 --> 00:52:55.274
to regenerate that output outside of the shiny application runtime and provide it to your end

00:52:55.274 --> 00:53:00.634
user in a way that they can just copy and paste that code into to a different R session.

